# VDSim — conventions & guardrails (any Cursor agent must follow)

Before working, read `docs/HANDOFF.md` (current state / next task) and the relevant
`docs/design/*.md` (the spec). Cursor-usage rules: `docs/design/CURSOR_USAGE.md`.

## Conventions
- Wheel index order: FL=0, FR=1, RL=2, RR=3 (all code/docs/plots).
- ISO 8855 right-handed frame (+x fwd, +y left, +z up).
- Estimation noise: Simon convention `Q = process noise`, `R = measurement noise`
  (NOT Thrun's R=process; Thrun only in SLAM-lecture context).
- Parameters are YAML-based (new configs follow the same pattern).
- Comments: default to none; only a short line for a non-obvious WHY. No what-comments,
  no multi-paragraph docstrings.

## Build & test (pass criteria)
- Build: `cmake --build build -j`
- Test: `cd build && ctest --output-on-failure` — **all 187 must stay green.**
- The default subsystem modules must reproduce current behaviour exactly (no
  re-baselining of ISO/benchmark numbers). If a number moves, stop and flag it.

## Git
- Never `push`, force-push, or move/retag without an explicit request.
- Small, focused commits; conventional messages; end with
  `Co-Authored-By: ...` only if appropriate. Do not commit unless asked, or as the
  documented end-of-task step.

## Security
- Hyundai (TUR) measured tire data is confidential. Never commit `.tir` values or
  details; refer to them generically only.

## Verification
- Do not guess external facts/APIs/numbers — verify, or say "unknown".
- Trust internal code/framework guarantees; validate only at real boundaries.

## Handoff
- When a task is done: build + ctest green, then update `docs/HANDOFF.md` so the next
  tool/session can continue. Final review/verification is Claude's responsibility.
